# Trino JVM - Banking Data Platform. Copy of the trinodb/trino:443 default
# jvm.config with the heap changed; mounted at /etc/trino/jvm.config in both the
# main and the CI compose files.
#
# The default heap is -XX:InitialRAMPercentage=80 / -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=80, i.e. 80%
# of the memory the JVM sees. On Docker Desktop (WSL2) the JVM does not see the
# container's cgroup limit, it sees the host: measured 2026-09-27, a container
# limited to 6000 MiB (memory.max 6291456000) ran with MaxHeapSize = 20166213632
# (~19 GB) and 10.3 GB of heap committed, and was OOM-killed by the kernel (exit
# 137, OOMKilled=true). A fixed heap does not depend on container detection.
#
# 4G of heap inside mem_limit 6000m leaves ~1.9 GB for everything off-heap
# (metaspace, 256M code cache, direct buffers for Parquet reads, thread stacks).
# tests/governance/test_jvm_heap_fits_container.py keeps that ratio if mem_limit or
# -Xmx changes. Keep this file ASCII: the launcher reads it, not Trino.
-server
-agentpath:/usr/lib/trino/bin/libjvmkill.so
-Xms1G
-Xmx4G
-XX:G1HeapRegionSize=32M
-XX:+ExplicitGCInvokesConcurrent
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
-XX:-OmitStackTraceInFastThrow
-XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize=256M
-XX:PerMethodRecompilationCutoff=10000
-XX:PerBytecodeRecompilationCutoff=10000
-Djdk.attach.allowAttachSelf=true
-Djdk.nio.maxCachedBufferSize=2000000
# Reduce starvation of threads by GClocker, recommend to set about the number of cpu cores (JDK-8192647)
-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions
-XX:GCLockerRetryAllocationCount=32
# Allow loading dynamic agent used by JOL
-XX:+EnableDynamicAgentLoading
